Let $y = x\arctan(x)$. Use the product rule to find the derivative:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 \cdot \arctan(x) + x \cdot \frac{1}{1+x^2} \\
&= \arctan(x) + \frac{x}{1+x^2}
\end{align*}$$
Find the gradient of the tangent at $x=1$:
$$\begin{align*}
m &= \arctan(1) + \frac{1}{1+1^2} \\
&= \frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{1}{2} \\
&= \frac{\pi+2}{4}
\end{align*}$$
Use the point-gradient formula with the point $\left(1,\frac{\pi}{4}\right)$:
$$\begin{align*}
y - y_1 &= m(x - x_1) \\
y - \frac{\pi}{4} &= \left(\frac{\pi+2}{4}\right)(x - 1) \\
y - \frac{\pi}{4} &= \left(\frac{\pi+2}{4}\right)x - \frac{\pi+2}{4} \\
y &= \left(\frac{\pi+2}{4}\right)x - \frac{\pi}{4} - \frac{2}{4} + \frac{\pi}{4} \\
y &= \frac{\pi+2}{4}x - \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}$$

Using the product rule:
$$\begin{align*}
y &= x\cos^{-1}x \\
\frac{dy}{dx} &= \cos^{-1}x+x\left(-\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\right) \\
&= \cos^{-1}x-\frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}
\end{align*}$$

From part $\textbf{(i)}$:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(x\cos^{-1}x\right) &= \cos^{-1}x-\frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} \\
\cos^{-1}x &= \frac{d}{dx}\left(x\cos^{-1}x\right)+\frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}
\end{align*}$$
Therefore,
$$\begin{align*}
\int \cos^{-1}x\,dx &= x\cos^{-1}x+\int \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx
\end{align*}$$
Let $u=1-x^2$. Then $du=-2x\,dx$.
$$\begin{align*}
\int \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx &= -\frac{1}{2}\int u^{-\frac{1}{2}}\,du \\
&= -u^{\frac{1}{2}} \\
&= -\sqrt{1-x^2}
\end{align*}$$
Hence,
$$\begin{align*}
\int \cos^{-1}x\,dx &= x\cos^{-1}x-\sqrt{1-x^2}+C
\end{align*}$$

$$\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{2x}{x^2+4}\right] &= \frac{2(x^2+4)-2x(2x)}{(x^2+4)^2} \\
&= \frac{8-2x^2}{(x^2+4)^2}
\end{align*}$$
Also:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)\right] &= \frac{1}{1+\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^2}\cdot \frac{1}{2} \\
&= \frac{2}{x^2+4}
\end{align*}$$
Therefore:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{2x}{x^2+4}+\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)\right] &= \frac{8-2x^2}{(x^2+4)^2}+\frac{2}{x^2+4} \\
&= \frac{8-2x^2+2x^2+8}{(x^2+4)^2} \\
&= \frac{16}{(x^2+4)^2}
\end{align*}$$
So $A=16$.

